<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="2" to="4" step="2"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="3" to="7" step="2"/>

  <function name="f">ln(1+$k*x^$a)</function>

</setup>

<p>
  Our goal in this exercise is to find the general antiderivative of the function
  <me>
    f(x) = \ln(1+$k x^$a)
  </me>
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
      Select which of the familiar Taylor series should be used and enter what should be substituted for the <m>x</m>.
        <choiceInput name="serieschoice" forAnswer="$seriesandsubs">
          <choice>
            <m> \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} x^k = 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + \cdots</m>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <m> \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} (-1)^{k-1} \frac{x^k}{k}  = x - \frac{1}{2}x^2 + \frac{1}{3}x^3 - \cdots</m>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <m> \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (-1)^{k} \frac{x^{2k+1}}{(2k+1)!} = x - \frac{1}{3!}x^3 + \frac{1}{5!}x^5 - \cdots</m>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <m> \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (-1)^{k} \frac{x^{2k}}{(2k)!} = 1 - \frac{1}{2!}x^2 + \frac{1}{4!}x^4 - \cdots</m>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <m> \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{x^k}{k!} = 1 + x + \frac{1}{2!}x^2 + \frac{1}{3!}x^3 + \cdots</m>
          </choice>
        </choiceInput>

        using <mathInput name="subschoice" forAnswer="$seriesandsubs"/> substituted for the <m>x</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer name="seriesandsubs"><award><when>$serieschoice.selectedIndex=2 and $subschoice=$k x^$a</when></award></answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the above series and subsitution, along with algebraic techniques if needed, to find the first four nonzero terms of the Taylor series centered at <m>0</m> for <m>f(x)</m>.  State those four respective terms in the provided blanks below.
      </p>
      <p sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
        <m>f(x) = </m><answer>$k*x^($a)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-1/2*$k^2*x^(2*$a)</answer><m>+</m><answer>1/3*$k^3*x^(3*$a)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-1/4*$k^4*x^(4*$a)</answer><m>+ \cdots \hspace{.1in}</m>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the open interval of convergence for the Taylor series of <m>f(x) = \ln(1+$k x^$a)</m>.  If the series converges for all real numbers, enter <m>(-\text{infinity}, \text{infinity})</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Taylor series of <m>f(x)</m> converges on <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>(-1/$k^(1/$a),1/$k^(1/$a))</answer>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use the Power Series Differentiation and Integration Theorem to find the first <em>five</em> nonzero terms of the Taylor series for <m>F(x)</m>, the general antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>.  Enter those terms in order of increasing degree in the blanks provided; if you need to include a constant of integration, use <q><m>C</m></q>.
      </p>
      <p sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
        <m>F(x) = </m><answer name="const">C</answer><m>+</m><answer>$k/($a+1)*x^($a+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-$k^2/(2*(2*$a+1))*x^(2*$a+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>$k^3/(3*(3*$a+1))*x^(3*$a+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-$k^4/(4*(4*$a+1))*x^(4*$a+1)</answer><m>+ \cdots \hspace{.1in}</m>
      </p>
      <feedback condition="$const=$k/($a+1)*x^($a+1)">The constant of integration needs to come first, and the <math simplify>$k/($a+1)*x^($a+1)</math> term will be second.</feedback>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the open interval of convergence for the Taylor series of <m>F(x)</m>.  If the series converges for all real numbers, enter <m>(-\text{infinity}, \text{infinity})</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Taylor series of <m>F(x)</m> converges on <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>(-1/$k^(1/$a),1/$k^(1/$a))</answer>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>